# M365 Copilot Readiness Assessment Tool - Service Principal Credentials
#
# This file is normally generated automatically by setup-service-principal.ps1.
# Copy this file to .env (Standard) or .env.restricted (Restricted) and fill in
# values only when configuring credentials manually. See docs/PERMISSIONS.md.
#
# DO NOT COMMIT THE REAL .env FILE TO GIT - it contains credentials.
# Closeout: see docs/CLEANUP.md. Removing this file alone does not revoke application grants.
# cleanup-service-principal.ps1 previews by default; its optional -RemoveEnvironmentFile
# removes only the selected configuration after successful remote cleanup and confirmation.
# Retain this configuration until identity checks and any cleanup retries are complete.

# Runtime permission profile: standard (default) or restricted.
# CLI --permission-profile overrides this setting for a live run.
# Restricted setup creates a dedicated "M365 Copilot Readiness Assessment Tool - Restricted"
# app and writes PERMISSION_PROFILE=restricted to .env.restricted. Select that file with
# --env-file .env.restricted. Changing this value does not revoke existing consent.
PERMISSION_PROFILE=standard

# Restricted retains stable identity/device/security/usage/external reads and omits
# site/group/consent-grant inventories and all SharePoint/Purview administrative PS.
# It rejects preview packs and legacy administrative collection. Use supported exports
# or saved evidence for additional coverage; omitted controls remain unassessed.

# Azure AD / Entra tenant ID (GUID) or domain (e.g., contoso.onmicrosoft.com)
TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Application (client) ID of the Entra app registration
CLIENT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Graph authentication - configure a usable option below.
# Administrative certificate-store thumbprints alone cannot authenticate Graph.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Option A (preferred): certificate authentication.
# Point at a .pem or .pfx holding the private key for a certificate uploaded to
# the app registration. Protect the certificate's private key and any password.
# CERTIFICATE_PATH=C:\path\to\assessment-cert.pfx
# CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=only-if-the-file-is-password-protected
# Initial setup -CertificatePath accepts an unprotected private-key PFX. Reusing an
# encrypted saved file requires matching existing tenant/client identity and a valid
# registered certificate. See docs/prereq.md for certificate-store thumbprint setup.

# Option B: client secret.
# Secret value for the app registration (setup-service-principal.ps1 defaults to
# a 90 day lifetime, so this needs periodic rotation).
CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-value
# Setup records the exact secret identifier and expiry for reuse and rotation checks.
# Leave these unset for a manually supplied legacy secret; setup verifies it before reuse.
# CLIENT_SECRET_KEY_ID=<credential-key-guid>
# CLIENT_SECRET_EXPIRES_AT=<UTC-expiration-timestamp>

# Optional authoritative data-exposure reports. Separate multiple paths with a
# semicolon on Windows. A directory imports every supported CSV, TSV, JSON, and
# XLSX report in that directory. Structured import discovery is not recursive;
# --reports-dir additionally discovers PDFs recursively in subfolders.
# SAM_DAG_REPORT_PATHS=C:\Reports\SAM\site-permissions.csv;C:\Reports\SAM\sharing-links.csv
# DSPM_REPORT_PATHS=C:\Reports\Purview\data-risk-assessment.csv

# Standard only: SharePoint governance. Restricted ignores administrative certificate
# settings and does not resolve the admin URL or start administrative PowerShell.
# Open the customer's Microsoft 365 admin center > Admin centers > SharePoint.
# Copy its actual HTTPS origin, e.g. https://<actual-prefix>-admin.sharepoint.com,
# without /_layouts/... or any query. Do not infer it from the initial tenant domain.
# Set this value (or pass --sharepoint-admin-url) for SharePoint administrative coverage.
# If missing, selected interactive collection can prompt; noninteractive runs leave
# SharePoint administration unassessed with a configuration gap, not a permission error.
SHAREPOINT_ADMIN_URL=

# SharePoint administrative PowerShell does not accept a client secret. For unattended
# collection, configure a PFX certificate uploaded to the same app registration. If these
# values are omitted, the SharePoint-only collector requests a delegated browser sign-in.
# SHAREPOINT_CERTIFICATE_PATH=C:\path\to\sharepoint-assessment.pfx
# SHAREPOINT_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=only-if-the-file-is-password-protected
# SHAREPOINT_CERTIFICATE_THUMBPRINT=certificate-thumbprint-in-current-user-store
# If using thumbprints, retain CERTIFICATE_PATH (and CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD if encrypted)
# or CLIENT_SECRET above for Graph; thumbprints do not replace that requirement.
# For fresh thumbprint setup, -RotateCredential explicitly creates the Graph secret.

# Standard only: certificate-first Purview collection. PURVIEW_ORGANIZATION is normally the initial
# tenant domain (for example contoso.onmicrosoft.com), not a SharePoint URL.
# PURVIEW_ORGANIZATION=contoso.onmicrosoft.com
# PURVIEW_CERTIFICATE_PATH=C:\path\to\purview-assessment.pfx
# PURVIEW_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=only-if-the-file-is-password-protected
# PURVIEW_CERTIFICATE_THUMBPRINT=certificate-thumbprint-in-current-user-store

# Optional specialized workloads (Insider Risk, Communication Compliance,
# Information Barriers, and eDiscovery) require separate roles. They are not
# queried during the normal assessment.
# PURVIEW_INCLUDE_SPECIALIZED=false

# Optional freshness policy. Permission/sharing reports and DSPM assessments use
# separate windows from content lifecycle reports. Packaged replay restores saved
# settings; --lifecycle-report-max-age-days explicitly overrides the lifecycle window.
# SAM_REPORT_MAX_AGE_DAYS=35
# DSPM_REPORT_MAX_AGE_DAYS=8
# LIFECYCLE_REPORT_MAX_AGE_DAYS=90
